When a vulnerability is found in a package, the risk level depends entirely on who can talk to that package.

Understanding how vulnerabilty appearing in different packages represent different threat to security

Express (The Exposed Front Door)
Express is a web server framework. Its entire purpose is to listen to the open internet and handle incoming requests from anyone, anywhere.

If Express has an RCE vulnerability, a hacker sitting across the world can send a specifically crafted HTTP request to your live server IP address.

Because Express automatically processes that request, the vulnerability triggers, and the hacker instantly gains access to your production database or server backend. You have zero control over who tries to exploit it.

ESLint (The Locked Private Office)
ESLint is a command-line tool that reads your local text files to check for syntax errors. It does not open a port to the internet. It does not listen for incoming network traffic.

For a hacker to exploit a vulnerability in ESLint, they would have to convince you to download a highly specific, malicious JavaScript file onto your computer, and then trick you into running ESLint on that specific file.

Because it only interacts with the files sitting on your local hard drive, the "attack surface" is practically zero unless your machine is already compromised.

Semantic Versioning (SemVer) is the universal language npm uses to manage package versions. It prevents your app from breaking when a dependency updates.
MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH 
1.   .   2 .  0. 

MAJOR (1): Breaking changes. Code written for version 1 might crash if upgraded to version 2.

MINOR (2): New features, but completely backward-compatible. Safe to upgrade.

PATCH (0): Backward-compatible bug fixes and optimizations. Extremely safe to upgrade.

Deciphering the Caret (^) and Tilde (~)
These prefixes tell npm how flexible it can be when you run npm update or npm install.

The Caret (^1.2.0)
The caret is the npm default. It says: "Give me bug fixes and new features, but do not update the major version because that will break my code."
^1.2.0 means: >=1.2.0 <2.0.0

What it allows: 1.2.1, 1.3.0, 1.9.5

What it blocks: 2.0.0 (it locks the 1)
The Tilde (~1.2.0)
The tilde is much more conservative. It says: "I only want bug fixes. Do not add new features, and definitely don't break anything."
~1.2.0 means: >=1.2.0 <1.3.0

What it allows: 1.2.1, 1.2.9

What it blocks: 1.3.0 (it locks the 1.2)

Exact Version (1.2.0)
If there is no symbol at all, it is locked down completely.
What it allows: Only 1.2.0. No updates ever, not even an urgent security patch.

The Wildcard (*)
This means absolute freedom (and absolute danger).

What it allows: Any version at all (equivalent to >=0.0.0). It will install the absolute latest release, even if it is a major breaking update like version 5.0.0. Avoid using this in production

