In standard JavaScript, if you try to read a property on something that is undefined or null, your code throws a fatal crash: TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined.

The optional chaining operator (?.) alters this behavior. It tells the JavaScript engine: "Look at the object on the left. If it exists, navigate inside it and grab the key. If it is null or undefined, stop searching immediately and just return undefined instead of crashing."

a?.vulns ?? []
While optional chaining stops your code from crashing, returning undefined introduces a secondary problem. If downstream code expects a real array to run a loop (like .forEach()), running a loop on undefined will still trigger a crash.

The nullish coalescing operator (??) provides a fallback value. It reads: "Look at the expression on my left side. If it evaluates to null or undefined, ignore it and swap it out for the fallback value on my right side."

